## Case: closure-hof-capture
id: "av.parity.1081.closure-hof-capture"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1081", "functions"]
links: ["PLAN-1081"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
fn each_count(f fn(int)int, xs List<int>) int {
    var n = 0
    for x in xs {
        let y = f(x)
        n = n + 1
    }
    return n
}
fn main() {
    let factor = 3
    let nums = [1, 2, 3]
    print(each_count((x int) => x * factor, nums))
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
channel: "unit"

#### stdout

```text
3
```

### Notes

legacy.09_functions.010_closure_hof_map (789d2b50777321ee) and
.013_closure_capture_hof (782d9cc0967a650c): the closure+capture
language face (a capturing closure passed as a HOF argument and applied
per element; the count oracle '3' matches both legacy documents).
`List.map` itself is a stdlib/HOF algorithm — research-hold rows point
at that owner; this case keeps the closure obligation on the ordinary
chain.

## Case: nested-fn-void
id: "av.parity.1081.nested-fn-void"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1081", "functions"]
links: ["PLAN-1081", "PLAN-514"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.at"

```auto
// Plan 514 W1-2 探针 d01：447-① 债复现件——嵌套 void fn 静默失效
// 正确语义：inner() 输出 "hi from inner"。
fn main() {
    fn inner() {
        print("hi from inner")
    }
    inner()
    print("after")
}
```

### Probe av
recipe: "ac-av/language-source-run-v1"
entry: "d_main"
caps: ["ac.source.language.av/v1", "console.stdout", "console.stderr"]
channel: "unit"

#### stdout

